ine lettuce","ingredient/romaine-lettuce"]]},{"h":"More from Cuisine of California","links":[["Green goddess dressing","dish/green-goddess-dressing"],["Hangtown fry","dish/hangtown-fry"],["Oysters Kirkpatrick","dish/oysters-kirkpatrick"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/ee/Celery_Victor_%287033758499%29.jpg/500px-Celery_Victor_%287033758499%29.jpg","w":500,"h":355,"by":"Katje Sabin from Chicago, IL, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celery_Victor_(7033758499).jpg"}},"dish/chicago-style-relish":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chicago-style-relish","n":"Chicago-style relish","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Midwestern American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Chicago-style relish is a type of pickle relish typically used on Chicago-style hot dogs. It is a sweet and tangy condiment made from finely chopped cucumbers, sugar, vinegar, and other spices. The unique color of the relish, often referred to as \"neon green\", is created by adding blue dye to regular pickle relish. The first use of Chicago-style relish on a hot dog has been attributed to several different restaurants, including Fluky's and Superdawg."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chicago-style-relish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago-style_relish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]},{"h":"More from Midwestern American cuisine","links":[["Pork tenderloin sandwich","dish/pork-tenderloin-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/02/GoldCoastChardog.jpg/500px-GoldCoastChardog.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"Wjmummert","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GoldCoastChardog.jpg"}},"dish/dalma-dish":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/dalma-dish","n":"Dalma (dish)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["The cuisine of Odisha is the cuisine of the Indian state of Odisha. Compared to other regional Indian cuisines, Odia cuisine uses less oil and is less spicy, while nonetheless remaining flavorful. Rice is the staple food of this region. Mustard oil is used in some dishes as the cooking medium, but ghee (made of cow's milk) is preferred in temples. Odia foods are traditionally served either on brass or bronze metal plates, banana leaves, or disposable plates made of sal leaves."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-dalma-dish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalma_(dish)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Odia cuisine","cuisine/odia-cuisine"],["Oriya cuisine","cuisine/oriya-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Away in the Kitchen (Odia home cooking)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"awayinthekitchen.com","note":"Blog section devoted to Odia home recipes.","url":"https://www.awayinthekitchen.com/odia-home-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Odia cuisine section of Archana Doshi's large Indian recipe site.","url":"https://archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/oriya","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Away in the Kitchen (Odia home cooking)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"awayinthekitchen.com","note":"Blog section devoted to Odia home recipes.","url":"https://www.awayinthekitchen.com/odia-home-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Odia cuisine section of Archana Doshi's large Indian recipe site.","url":"https://archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/oriya","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Odia cuisine","links":[["Dahibara Aloodum","dish/dahibara-aloodum"],["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/13/Dalama_Odia_Cuisine.jpg/500px-Dalama_Odia_Cuisine.jpg","w":500,"h":364,"by":"Subhashish Panigrahi","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalama_Odia_Cuisine.jpg"}},"dish/japanese-noodles":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/japanese-noodles","n":"Japanese noodles","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Noodles are a staple of Japanese cuisine. They are often served chilled with dipping sauces, or in soups or hot dishes. Noodles were introduced to Japan from China during the Song Dynasty between the Heian until the early Kamakura period."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-japanese-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Curry bread","dish/curry-bread"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Japanese c